**Claim.** Yes, $F$ is necessarily fully faithful.

**Proof.**

Recall that a $\mathcal{V}$-functor $H:\mathcal{A}\to\mathcal{B}$ is **fully faithful in the enriched sense** if for every pair of objects $a,a'\in\mathcal{A}$, the structure morphism
$$H_{a,a'}:\mathcal{A}(a,a')\longrightarrow\mathcal{B}(Ha,Ha')$$
is an isomorphism in the underlying category of $\mathcal{V}$.

We are given:
- $G\circ F$ is fully faithful, so for all $c,c'\in\mathcal{C}$, the morphism
$$(G\circ F)_{c,c'}:\mathcal{C}(c,c')\longrightarrow\mathcal{E}(GFc,GFc')$$
is an isomorphism in $\mathcal{V}$.
- $G$ is fully faithful, so for all $d,d'\in\mathcal{D}$, the morphism
$$G_{d,d'}:\mathcal{D}(d,d')\longrightarrow\mathcal{E}(Gd,Gd')$$
is an isomorphism in $\mathcal{V}$.

By the definition of composition of $\mathcal{V}$-functors, the action of $G\circ F$ on hom-objects factors as:
$$\mathcal{C}(c,c')\xrightarrow{\;F_{c,c'}\;}\mathcal{D}(Fc,Fc')\xrightarrow{\;G_{Fc,Fc'}\;}\mathcal{E}(GFc,GFc'),$$
that is,
$$(G\circ F)_{c,c'}=G_{Fc,Fc'}\circ F_{c,c'}.$$

Since $G$ is fully faithful and $Fc,Fc'$ are objects of $\mathcal{D}$, the morphism $G_{Fc,Fc'}$ is an isomorphism in $\mathcal{V}$. Since $G\circ F$ is fully faithful, the composite $(G\circ F)_{c,c'}=G_{Fc,Fc'}\circ F_{c,c'}$ is an isomorphism in $\mathcal{V}$.

Now we apply the **2-out-of-3 property for isomorphisms**, which holds in any category (in particular, in the underlying category of $\mathcal{V}$): if $g$ and $g\circ f$ are both isomorphisms, then $f=g^{-1}\circ(g\circ f)$ is a composition of two isomorphisms, hence an isomorphism.

Setting $f=F_{c,c'}$ and $g=G_{Fc,Fc'}$, we conclude that
$$F_{c,c'}:\mathcal{C}(c,c')\longrightarrow\mathcal{D}(Fc,Fc')$$
is an isomorphism in $\mathcal{V}$ for every pair $c,c'\in\mathcal{C}$.

Therefore $F$ is fully faithful in the enriched sense. $\blacksquare$

